{
  "id": "proj-leather-tool-roll",
  "slug": "leather-tool-roll",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Leather Tool Roll",
  "summary": "A roll-up pouch for chisels or wrenches. Big panels, straight stitches, very forgiving.",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedHours": 3,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Mark out the panels on the flesh side with the ruler and the awl point of the divider: a 16 in x 12 in body and a 16 in x 6 in pocket strip.",
    "Cut them on the cutting mat with several light passes of the knife against the ruler, rather than one hard pass. Round the corners by tracing a coin.",
    "Bevel every edge that will show, on both sides.",
    "Scuff the grain side of the body where the pocket will sit, then glue the pocket strip along its bottom and side edges with a thin line of contact cement.",
    "Run the divider 3/16 in in from the edge to mark the stitch line. Lay your tools on the pocket and mark a line between each pair for the pocket dividers, leaving room for each tool's thickness.",
    "On the punching board, punch the stitch holes through both layers with the stitching chisels, using the single prong around the corners.",
    "Saddle stitch: cut thread about four times the seam length, put a needle on each end, and pass both needles through every hole from opposite sides. Backstitch two holes at each end.",
    "Stitch each pocket divider from the bottom up and backstitch at the top of the pocket, where the strain is.",
    "Dampen the edges, rub in the edge gum, and burnish them hard with the slicker until they shine.",
    "Punch two holes near the end of the body, thread the lace through, then roll it up and tie it."
  ],
